.\" Keep in step with print_usage() and the header comment in
.\" src/bin/hkdfguard.rs.
.TH HKDFGUARD 1 2026-11-17 "hkdfguard 0.9.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hkdfguard \- provision a service's HKDFGuard KEK, and wrap DEKs and secrets under it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hkdfguard provision
.BR \-\-service\-name | \-sn
.I name
.PP
.B hkdfguard wrap
.BR \-\-key\-file\-path | \-kf
.I path
.BR \-\-service\-name | \-sn
.I name
.RB ( \-\-dek\-stdin " | " \-\-dek\-file
.IR path )
.RB [ \-\-group | \-g
.IR group ]
.RB [ \-\-fingerprint | \-fp
.IR hex ]
.RB [ \-\-force | \-f ]
.PP
.B hkdfguard protect
.BR \-\-service\-name | \-sn
.I name
.BR \-\-secret\-name | \-sid
.I name
.RB ( \-\-secret\-stdin " | " \-\-secret\-file
.IR path )
.RB [ \-\-base64 ]
.RB [ \-\-fingerprint | \-fp
.IR hex ]
.RB ( \-\-key\-file\-path | \-kf
.I path
.RB [ \-\-group | \-g
.IR group ]
.RB [ \-\-force | \-f "] | " \-\-stdout )
.PP
.B hkdfguard retire
.BR \-\-service\-name | \-sn
.I name
.BR \-\-fingerprint | \-fp
.I hex
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hkdfguard
sets up and uses the persistent, per-service Key Encryption Key (KEK) of
the HKDFGuard library. The KEK lives on the strongest provider that the
host has and that policy allows: a TPM 2.0, a PKCS#11 token, or an
external secret file.
.TP
.B provision
Ensures a KEK exists for the service, creating one if it does not, and
prints its fingerprint: record it.
Run it once, at deployment time. With
.BR retire ,
it is the only command that makes the library's setup calls, which are
deliberately slow. Under the production security model, creating a KEK
needs root; for an existing one, anyone may run it to verify the KEK.
.TP
.B wrap
Wraps a 32-byte Data Encryption Key (DEK) under the service's existing
KEK and writes the wrapped payload to
.IR \-\-key\-file\-path ,
mode 0600 (0640 with
.BR \-\-group ),
and prints the fingerprint of the KEK it wrapped under. It
never creates a KEK: if none has been provisioned, it fails and says to run
.BR provision .
Under the production security model it needs root or a deployer, and
refuses anyone else before reading the DEK.
.TP
.B protect
.B wrap
for a secret: a password or other credential of 1 to 256 bytes, wrapped
under the service's existing KEK and bound to
.IR \-\-secret\-name ,
which must be given again to unwrap it. A payload wrapped for one secret
name does not unwrap under another. Every secret is padded to the same
length, so the payload does not reveal how long the secret is.
.IP
With
.IR \-\-key\-file\-path ,
the payload is written to a key file exactly as
.B wrap
writes one, for an application to read with
.BR hkdfguard_open_secret_file (),
and under the production security model it needs root or a deployer.
With
.BR \-\-stdout ,
it is printed as base64 instead, for the caller to store; nothing on disk
is checked then, and whoever stores it must keep it writable only by root
or a deployer: anyone who can replace the payload, and can wrap under the
service's KEK, can substitute a secret they know.
.TP
.B retire
Permanently deletes the service's KEK, only if its fingerprint is the one
given; every DEK and secret wrapped under it can no longer be unwrapped.
TPM2 and PKCS#11 only: an external secret belongs to the platform that
mounts it, and an ephemeral KEK ends with its process. For a TPM KEK it
deletes the service's key file and authValue file; a backup of both would
still load on the same TPM. Under the production security model it needs
root.
.SH SECURITY MODEL
The policy's top-level
.B security_model
setting is
.B production
(the default) or
.BR developer .
Production splits the duties: root, the security admin, runs
.B provision
and
.BR retire ;
root or a deployer, the deployment admin, runs
.B wrap
and
.BR "protect \-\-key\-file\-path" ;
the application only unwraps. A deployer is a direct member of the group
.B hkdfguard\-deployers
(the packages create it) or of a group listed in the policy's
.BR deployer_groups .
The library trusts only key files owned by root or a deployer, in folders
owned by root or a deployer and group-writable only through a deployer
group; any other is refused with
.BR HKDFGUARD_ERR_FILE_UNTRUSTED .
Under developer, anyone who can reach the provider may do all of these, and
key files owned by the reading account are trusted. Wrapping and
unwrapping themselves are the same under both.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-service\-name ", " \-sn " \fIname\fR"
The service whose KEK is used. At most 128 bytes of ASCII letters,
digits and dots; it must not start with a dot or contain "..".
.TP
.BR \-\-key\-file\-path ", " \-kf " \fIpath\fR"
Where
.B wrap
or
.B protect
writes the wrapped payload. Its folder must be a real folder (no
symbolic link anywhere above the file), and neither that folder nor any
folder above it up to
.I /
may be writable by group or others \(em sticky folders such as
.IR /tmp ,
.I /var/tmp
and
.I /dev/shm
included. The library refuses to read a key file under such a folder
.RB ( HKDFGUARD_ERR_FILE_UNTRUSTED ),
so neither command writes one there, and both refuse before reading the
DEK or secret. Under the developer security model only the folders' modes
are checked, not who owns them; under production each folder must also be
owned by root or a deployer, and may be group-writable only through a
deployer group.
.TP
.B \-\-dek\-stdin
Read the DEK, base64 of exactly 32 bytes, from standard input. Refused
when standard input is a terminal, where the DEK would be echoed.
.TP
.BI \-\-dek\-file " path"
Read the DEK, base64 of exactly 32 bytes, from a file. The file must be a
regular file, not a symlink, owned by root or by the invoking user, with
no group or other access (mode 0400 or 0600). Its folder, and every folder
above it up to
.IR / ,
must be a real folder owned by root or the invoking user and not writable
by group or others \(em sticky folders such as
.I /tmp
included, unless sticky and on a read-only mount. No POSIX ACL entry
may give anyone else access to the file, or write access to a folder.
.TP
.BR \-\-secret\-name ", " \-sid " \fIname\fR"
For
.BR protect :
the name the secret is bound to, such as
.B vault.password
or
.BR ssl.certificate .
1 to 64 bytes of lowercase ASCII letters, digits,
.BR . ,
.B _
and
.BR \- ;
no uppercase, so two spellings that look alike can't name different keys.
The application gives the same name to unwrap it.
.TP
.B \-\-secret\-stdin
For
.BR protect :
read the secret from standard input. Refused when standard input is a
terminal.
.TP
.BI \-\-secret\-file " path"
For
.BR protect :
read the secret from a file, held to the same rules as
.BR \-\-dek\-file .
.TP
.B \-\-base64
For
.BR protect :
the input is base64 of the secret rather than the secret itself. Needed
for a binary secret, or one that ends in a newline.
.TP
.B \-\-stdout
For
.BR protect :
print the wrapped secret as base64 on standard output instead of writing a
key file. Give exactly one of
.B \-\-stdout
and
.BR \-\-key\-file\-path .
.TP
.BR \-\-group ", " \-g " \fIgroup\fR"
Give the key file to
.I group
(a name or a numeric gid), mode 0640: its owner may read and write it, and
the group's members may read it. Without it the key file is mode 0600,
readable by its owner only. A group that covers essentially every account
or the host's administrators \(em
.BR root ,
.BR users ,
.BR nogroup ,
.BR nobody ,
.BR wheel ,
.BR sudo ,
.BR adm ,
.BR staff ,
gid 0 or gid 65534 \(em is refused. With
.BR protect ,
only with
.BR \-\-key\-file\-path .
.TP
.BR \-\-force ", " \-f
Replace an existing
.IR \-\-key\-file\-path .
The new payload is written to a temporary file in the same folder first,
then renamed over the old file in one step, so a failed wrap or write
never touches it. The replaced file is then overwritten; that cannot
guarantee erasure on copy-on-write filesystems or flash storage. A file
this account may not write to is replaced without being overwritten, and
a warning says so. With
.BR protect ,
only with
.BR \-\-key\-file\-path .
.TP
.BR \-\-fingerprint ", " \-fp " \fIhex\fR"
The KEK's fingerprint as
.B provision
printed it, 64 hex characters. For
.B wrap
and
.BR protect ,
optional: nothing is written unless the payload was wrapped under that
KEK, and with
.B \-\-force
the existing file is left as it was. For
.BR retire ,
required: nothing is deleted unless it matches.
.TP
.BR \-\-help ", " \-h
Print usage and exit.
.PP
Neither the DEK nor a secret is ever accepted as a command-line argument
or from an environment variable: other processes of the same user can
read both through
.IR /proc .
A single trailing newline (LF or CRLF) on the DEK or secret input is
ignored.
.PP
Each option may be given once, and an option's value may not start with
.BR \- :
.B "wrap \-kf \-\-force"
is an invalid command line, not a key file named
.BR \-\-force .
Write a path that starts with
.B \-
as
.IR ./\-name .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
The operation failed, or the process could not be hardened (core dumps,
ptrace, memory locking) and refused to handle key material.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command line.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/hkdfguard/policy.toml
Administrative policy: which providers may be used, and their
configuration. Required: without it every call fails. It must be owned by root and not
writable by group or others, and so must every directory above it up to
.IR / ,
sticky ones such as
.I /tmp
included. The same holds for every file it names: nothing the library
reads is trusted anywhere under
.IR /tmp ,
.I /var/tmp
or
.IR /dev/shm ;
only a sticky directory on a read-only mount (a Kubernetes Secret or
projected volume) is exempt. POSIX ACL entries
are held to the same rules as the mode bits.
.TP
.I /usr/share/hkdfguard/policy.toml.example
Annotated example policy, every setting commented out at its default.
Copy it to
.I /etc/hkdfguard/policy.toml
to start one.
.TP
.I /var/lib/hkdfguard/tpm/
Default TPM key directory (the policy's
.BR tpm.key_dir ).
Each TPM-backed service has a
.IB service .tpmkey
file, its key as only this TPM can load it, and a
.IB service .tpmauth
file, the authValue that authorizes the key. The key file takes the
directory's group; the authValue file the service's own group from the
policy's
.BR tpm.auth_groups ,
or root alone. Back up both together.
.TP
.I /etc/hkdfguard/pkcs11.pin
Default PKCS#11 user PIN file.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/sysusers.d/hkdfguard.conf
Creates the groups
.B hkdfguard
and
.BR hkdfguard\-deployers ,
which are never removed.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
sudo hkdfguard provision \-sn com.company.orders
printf '%s' "$DEK_B64" | sudo hkdfguard wrap \e
    \-kf /var/lib/app/key.bin \-sn com.company.orders \-\-dek\-stdin \-g app
hkdfguard wrap \-kf /var/lib/app/key.bin \e
    \-sn com.company.orders \-\-dek\-file "$CREDENTIALS_DIRECTORY/dek"
printf '%s' "$PASS" | sudo hkdfguard protect \-sn com.company.orders \e
    \-sid ssl.certificate \-\-secret\-stdin \-kf /var/lib/app/cert.pass \-g app
hkdfguard protect \-sn com.company.orders \-sid vault.password \e
    \-\-secret\-file /run/secrets/vault \-\-stdout
hkdfguard retire \-sn com.company.orders \-fp <fingerprint from provision>
.fi
.PP
.B printf
is a shell builtin, so neither the DEK nor the secret ever appears in a
process's argument list; an external
.B echo
would put it there.
.SH SEE ALSO
The HKDFGuard README, installed under
.IR /usr/share/doc/ ,
documents providers, the policy file and deployment.
